#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125282} :activity/id #uuid "4335d62f-6a14-5864-be7f-c04874251357" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125282 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125729 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $f(x,y) = x y^2 - x^3 y.$ Then the first-order partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} (x,y) = y^2 -3x^2y \\quad \\mathrm{and} \\quad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x,y) = 2xy -x^3,\n$$\n\nand the second-order **mixed partial derivatives** are \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial y\\, \\partial x}(x,y) = 2y -3x^2 \\quad \\mathrm{and} \\quad  \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x\\, \\partial y}(x,y) = 2y-3x^2.\n$$\n\nNotice that these two mixed partial derivatives are equal, i.e., \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial y\\, \\partial x}=\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x\\, \\partial y}.\n$$\n\nIn general, if a function $f(x,y)$ has continuous first-order partial derivatives on an open set $U,$ and the second-order mixed partial derivatives exist and are continuous on $U,$ then the mixed partial derivatives are equal everywhere on $U.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "96f3873b-8f1b-5481-827e-503e557bbe9c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2408 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "81952bcf-8d7d-50aa-9c3d-0f75127d5ba3" :step/math-academy-id 10705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125283}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125283 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124448 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124252 :question/id #uuid "2247dc40-0c71-596c-9da4-a3bffd85e106" :question/math-academy-id "e-3152"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ee40fd-e452-5593-9a35-7c46d1f0cca0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048781}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573326}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Constants in First-Order Partial Derivative Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "3cac5a52-91a4-530b-93a7-3b880c3d7869" :step/math-academy-id 10709 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125285}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125284 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124449 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124253 :question/id #uuid "d45926a5-0126-50b4-bdc7-a9f686408986" :question/math-academy-id "e-3153"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7619454-7a2b-5c0b-927c-24dca2e564c3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573251}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions of Partial Derivatives at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "03eb0268-bd36-5a13-83fe-9c238bbe818f" :step/math-academy-id 10710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125285 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124254 :question/id #uuid "d62e8502-b3c5-5923-8ab3-ef988507cd2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3154"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532690}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "e1813dc3-f991-54bc-bf74-b3e674f858b0" :step/math-academy-id 10713 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125330}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125755 :tutorial/content "If $f(x,y)$ is a function whose first-order and second-order mixed partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ then the second-order mixed partial derivatives are equal everywhere on $U.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x\\, \\partial y} =\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial y\\, \\partial x}\n$$\n\nThis result also extends to higher-order partial derivatives. For example, if the third-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ are also continuous everywhere on $U,$ then the third-order mixed partial derivatives are independent of the order in which the differentiation is performed. \n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{blue}\\partial x\\, \\partial x\\, \\partial y} =\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{red}\\partial y\\, \\partial x \\, \\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can think of $\\color{blue}\\partial x\\, \\partial x\\, \\partial y$ and $\\color{red}\\partial y\\, \\partial x \\, \\partial x$ as distinct permutations of the symbols $\\partial x,\\partial x,$ and $\\partial y.$ Any permutation of these symbols gives the same result. For example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{blue}\\partial x\\, \\partial x\\, \\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{purple}\\partial x\\, \\partial y\\, \\partial x}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** This does **not** mean that *all* third partial derivatives are equal! For example, it is generally true that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{blue}\\partial x\\, \\partial x\\, \\partial y} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\partial x\\, \\partial y\\, \\partial y}.\n$$\n\n \nThese are not equal because $\\partial x\\, \\partial y\\, \\partial y$ (in the right-hand side denominator) is a permutation of the symbols $\\partial x, \\partial y, \\partial y,$ not $\\partial x, \\partial x, \\partial y.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2267ab49-4a4d-553c-ba46-127edac4a2e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2884 :tutorial/title "Equality of Higher-Order Partial Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "fec110b5-a92e-591e-b9da-4dceefec8548" :step/math-academy-id 15480 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125284}}] :activity/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124742}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409654} :activity/id #uuid "3c715a18-b04f-5fa8-b565-cd1d87a53bb9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4421a8c6-aea6-5d62-9cd0-8a06f8915dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409638} {:answer/id #uuid "45730328-f0dc-5c33-a6c2-53d71d04445d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409639} {:answer/id #uuid "4d58c242-f6fd-5d67-8e96-0273fcae35a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409641} {:answer/id #uuid "59fd5070-c975-5965-b9d4-d62ba5834853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409642} {:answer/id #uuid "d26ca855-4a28-50fd-bba1-040fec87c89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409642} :answer-field/id #uuid "504a276b-9fed-5959-a86c-c2b987992901" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "923e8280-3481-5e90-8081-225a78ef2cea" :question/math-academy-id "q-133393" :question/problem "Evaluate ${f}_{zxz}(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ given that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n{f}_{zz}(x,y,z)=2z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+x{e}^{z-2y}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zzx}(x,y,z) & ={f}_{zxz}(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{zz}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+x{e}^{z-2y}) \\\\  & =2z\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2y}+{e}^{z-2y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2z}{x-2y}+{e}^{z-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point  $(2,\\frac{1}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zxz}(2,\\frac{1}{2},1) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}+{e}^{1-2\\cdot 1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-1}+{e}^{1-1} \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "035a465c-a1c6-5467-ae17-a4aa635d3e19"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad486eb1-3af5-599d-a9fa-a159211f22e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469206} {:answer/id #uuid "34be447a-67c4-5a73-8480-4c9e222a6cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469207}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469206} :answer-field/id #uuid "25cbfe12-d294-5245-8577-5393288ad9f4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470215} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b396de7c-837a-5a29-89f1-49cbd1530aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469208} {:answer/id #uuid "8cec73c4-a0c4-5d0f-b9fe-a203948ac871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469209} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8af40fc-fffa-522e-a59f-f12c36507ded" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470216} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcdf7232-c17f-5a4f-aed3-4cd3d5ebaa84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469210} {:answer/id #uuid "45e16277-28a2-510b-88f0-c7b6217947e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469211}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469211} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6521383-885b-5c99-af14-d69ae9a433ee" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0fedf8ed-0c48-5105-b489-f0efda76d273" :question/math-academy-id "q-80759" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n- **I.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{-2x}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{-y}-2x$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=5$\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-2x}-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-y}-2x)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2})=4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy) \\\\  & =2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."} :step/id #uuid "55584015-c136-5819-9111-6b2b98328a75" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "537b62fe-d484-51a3-9b9d-3eac384ae86b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459871} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb00923-d31f-50a0-8430-a21e4ada7116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459872}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459871} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff56698d-432b-5f8c-a2f0-aff9cac4c567" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460802} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab765baa-eeb1-592d-b1f7-a75e1fec7c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459873} {:answer/id #uuid "49651fcd-eecc-5a62-ac69-569a5ab448f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459874}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459874} :answer-field/id #uuid "d032d783-9324-58fd-b325-e0e4ffdbb557" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460803} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45153bf2-25d4-5bce-aa67-9b8cdbe22edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459875} {:answer/id #uuid "8fe20793-5aef-5f1d-a189-e87f42839efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459876}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459876} :answer-field/id #uuid "108ed4b8-6596-5afb-b079-663c97064ef5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "574e3825-54df-5b43-b74f-6544e0543982" :question/math-academy-id "q-36518" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n- **I.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x+4y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3x-y$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=0$\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+4y)=4 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-y)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."} :step/id #uuid "87e1e468-067a-5b37-817d-ee60c3d401bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a2bcb36-868f-5f54-a6d3-5044da4b78ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409640} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1bdf62-5e9d-55fa-82ff-dab50af9bc96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409643} {:answer/id #uuid "70500de8-c6d4-538a-a490-a042ef069ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409644} {:answer/id #uuid "92afcd1e-56b0-514b-aa96-d6441ab63941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409645} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d893f9-d5e3-5e3f-8ad1-b21394af5064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409646} :answer-field/id #uuid "c178518b-a0fe-5bb1-9ccb-2e562d423be4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "08ef8961-263a-5251-a28f-91bd26031d10" :question/math-academy-id "q-36523" :question/problem "Consider that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first, second and third-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ What is the value of ${f}_{yxy}(1,0)-{f}_{yy}(1,0)-{f}_{xyy}(1,0)$ given that ${f}_{yy}(x,y)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy?$" :question/worked-solution "As the function and its first, second, and third-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ we must have ${f}_{yxy}(x,y)={f}_{xyy}(x,y).$ \n\nTherefore, we can swap the order of the subscripts in the first term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(1,0)-{f}_{yy}(1,0)-{f}_{xyy}(1,0) & ={f}_{xyy}(1,0)-{f}_{yy}(1,0)-{f}_{xyy}(1,0) \\\\  & =\\cancel{{f}_{xyy}(1,0)}-{f}_{yy}(1,0)-\\cancel{{f}_{xyy}(1,0)} \\\\  & =-{f}_{yy}(1,0) \\\\  & =-(1)^{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "95f751f2-5411-50b2-8387-307a1ef77bce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18f0539-74a8-596c-852b-0d4f3da74f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471633} {:answer/id #uuid "da87614c-db0e-5e0d-85a3-4f809e951445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471634}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471633} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5174ed-11cb-5dac-8790-f507ecda6be1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472322} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc7392ed-77ee-5baa-b9bc-16067d951374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471635} {:answer/id #uuid "fc39236e-b074-5bb4-8887-3fc17013ce23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471636}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471636} :answer-field/id #uuid "a26127c3-e674-5d2b-a403-9fdb7056fadf" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472323} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "614463b9-6217-51ba-a62e-5077088acdf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471637} {:answer/id #uuid "beab60f2-37b0-54a2-8506-e97f08da4b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471638}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471637} :answer-field/id #uuid "f171c56b-5039-5e7e-825f-8cf0186be360" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "24df999b-6ea6-5095-ada6-d510b0d36076" :question/math-academy-id "q-80652" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n- **I.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=4x+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=4xy-y$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}y+2x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-y)=4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{y})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2x+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+2x)=2xy+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."} :step/id #uuid "dd83cd33-8573-5ef5-a0d5-36235eb60d0a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409653}}] :activity/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409637}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481716} :activity/id #uuid "3c12d8df-59d7-51f9-9588-92c3d42e2238" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002afc6b-3ae6-5e4d-9e87-6404be2be05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479355} {:answer/id #uuid "a95258ee-6056-5703-bec4-79d09d5e79de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479610} {:answer/id #uuid "d3510268-8c4c-529e-8b03-2fd8c635e27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479679} {:answer/id #uuid "dd42a3d6-7453-56cc-b6fd-7e6f716d0a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479686} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc862f5-359e-590e-930c-858b88ff0363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479689}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479686} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff4a0306-bebb-5a0b-88e8-580bcd8a3741" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0c2a62d8-6d93-5394-bb23-b474c6edf899" :question/math-academy-id "q-65128" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2y+1$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2y+1$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(1+xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y+1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."} :step/id #uuid "0330742a-33ff-5afc-89cc-0f92fa6fe78f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481724}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481716 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3e26fd3-6f10-57ac-9734-b2dbccddcc7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385358} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca4c3e9-3be8-5181-ba90-8364eb252764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385359} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d7bc4e-c57e-5018-af71-503efedc8f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385360} {:answer/id #uuid "be8924f2-9c7d-5be9-8109-8089f340a29f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385361} {:answer/id #uuid "2094e71e-1eb0-5d91-a373-c7101206e62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385362}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385361} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6caccd5-2239-5eb0-a506-3902ad0cde8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8d93fe54-16d4-572d-b03f-303a7e6e19a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-36521" :question/problem "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}=(x{y}^{2}+2y){e}^{xy}+2{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}=6x{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nHence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x{y}^{2}+2y){e}^{xy}+2{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =(x{y}^{2}+2y)\\cdot x{e}^{xy}+(2xy+2)\\cdot {e}^{xy}+0 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4xy+2){e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6x{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =12xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,0,1)} & =(0+0+2){e}^{0}=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,0,1)} & =12(1)(1)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,0,1)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,0,1)} & =2+12=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7f5bbb0a-88b6-5f7d-9f06-8a26f6ee53a3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f5ce90-f71e-52dc-8beb-408f7faeaa27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479415} {:answer/id #uuid "48a543dd-5073-5eff-a396-7a9535994086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479465} {:answer/id #uuid "4a8aa56b-9b6e-5e43-81d5-2db6976aeebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479471} {:answer/id #uuid "684701da-26b3-560c-8537-f88180932fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479514} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb1a895-8ba1-56dc-9e30-7108e7d80a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479415} :answer-field/id #uuid "50f2ed47-78e7-59d4-a40f-57fb0a2f9402" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "87b5b3e0-dcda-563d-b53d-0df7f66aab12" :question/math-academy-id "q-65129" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxy}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ if ${f}_{yy}(x,y)=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(x,y) & ={f}_{yyx}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+(-{x}^{2})\\cdot (-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxy}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+(1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\,\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a01dfe7c-004d-5b4b-a3ba-f0b7986950c5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481744}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4421a8c6-aea6-5d62-9cd0-8a06f8915dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409638} {:answer/id #uuid "45730328-f0dc-5c33-a6c2-53d71d04445d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409639} {:answer/id #uuid "4d58c242-f6fd-5d67-8e96-0273fcae35a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409641} {:answer/id #uuid "59fd5070-c975-5965-b9d4-d62ba5834853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409642} {:answer/id #uuid "d26ca855-4a28-50fd-bba1-040fec87c89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409642} :answer-field/id #uuid "504a276b-9fed-5959-a86c-c2b987992901" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "923e8280-3481-5e90-8081-225a78ef2cea" :question/math-academy-id "q-133393" :question/problem "Evaluate ${f}_{zxz}(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ given that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n{f}_{zz}(x,y,z)=2z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+x{e}^{z-2y}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zzx}(x,y,z) & ={f}_{zxz}(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{zz}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+x{e}^{z-2y}) \\\\  & =2z\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2y}+{e}^{z-2y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2z}{x-2y}+{e}^{z-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point  $(2,\\frac{1}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zxz}(2,\\frac{1}{2},1) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}+{e}^{1-2\\cdot 1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-1}+{e}^{1-1} \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dce668a8-ef22-5a60-bb9c-32f6ba050503"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad486eb1-3af5-599d-a9fa-a159211f22e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469206} {:answer/id #uuid "34be447a-67c4-5a73-8480-4c9e222a6cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469207}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469206} :answer-field/id #uuid "25cbfe12-d294-5245-8577-5393288ad9f4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470215} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b396de7c-837a-5a29-89f1-49cbd1530aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469208} {:answer/id #uuid "8cec73c4-a0c4-5d0f-b9fe-a203948ac871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469209} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8af40fc-fffa-522e-a59f-f12c36507ded" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470216} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcdf7232-c17f-5a4f-aed3-4cd3d5ebaa84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469210} {:answer/id #uuid "45e16277-28a2-510b-88f0-c7b6217947e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469211}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469211} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6521383-885b-5c99-af14-d69ae9a433ee" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0fedf8ed-0c48-5105-b489-f0efda76d273" :question/math-academy-id "q-80759" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n- **I.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{-2x}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{-y}-2x$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=5$\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-2x}-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-y}-2x)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2})=4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy) \\\\  & =2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."} :step/id #uuid "efa110b9-bada-584b-9f76-02913ac29d55" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481739}}] :activity/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481568}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489359} :activity/id #uuid "c50fc124-2e70-50d8-bf4f-018c42fcb65d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6c9d7c-6f8b-549f-ade8-d1e4ebbf0134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266377} {:answer/id #uuid "2db15dbd-e51f-532f-9bfd-157b9f779945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266548} {:answer/id #uuid "3c660db5-3c31-5643-8113-78583f356468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266674} {:answer/id #uuid "a5eecdbb-ca77-56e2-9dcd-5a6810654073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267646} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a5f3a4-eed9-50d5-8e07-a411ebbe09d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266377} :answer-field/id #uuid "50bb18b4-57b1-5ebe-9423-935bc3b0a3cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9379a450-3ef7-54fa-b6cb-c35ea0f78eee" :question/math-academy-id "q-133382" :question/problem "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}=\\frac{x{e}^{yz}}{x+y},\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}=-\\frac{z{e}^{yz}}{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(1,1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂x∂y}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nHence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x{e}^{yz}}{x+y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{yz}\\cdot (\\frac{1\\cdot (x+y)-x\\cdot 1}{(x+y)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{yz}}{(x+y)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-\\frac{z{e}^{yz}}{x+y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot ({e}^{yz}+yz{e}^{yz}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{yz}(1+yz)}{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}}{(1+1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =-\\frac{{e}^{1\\cdot 0}(1+1\\cdot 0)}{1+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,1,0)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "769313f6-43dc-5919-9553-b36396031990"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489350 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002afc6b-3ae6-5e4d-9e87-6404be2be05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479355} {:answer/id #uuid "a95258ee-6056-5703-bec4-79d09d5e79de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479610} {:answer/id #uuid "d3510268-8c4c-529e-8b03-2fd8c635e27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479679} {:answer/id #uuid "dd42a3d6-7453-56cc-b6fd-7e6f716d0a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479686} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc862f5-359e-590e-930c-858b88ff0363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479689}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479686} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff4a0306-bebb-5a0b-88e8-580bcd8a3741" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0c2a62d8-6d93-5394-bb23-b474c6edf899" :question/math-academy-id "q-65128" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2y+1$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2y+1$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(1+xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y+1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."} :step/id #uuid "aae4262b-4042-5b50-9965-2172062f55a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f60ae37-1165-5440-bde6-f01cffce801b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266987} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8382b2-cf1c-5bb5-b534-beacbde2bdfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267414} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b8eef4-ff75-5267-860e-a762c49305b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267662} {:answer/id #uuid "c4658edd-5348-55cc-a2aa-5ce494911f0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267898} {:answer/id #uuid "f3239213-7c2d-5989-a3bf-a19a7229c158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268307}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267414} :answer-field/id #uuid "a30a187b-9301-5273-a095-e7add20e9085" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "33686134-3f7d-5a05-9109-0d2ac79b776f" :question/math-academy-id "q-133373" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxy}(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ if ${f}_{xy}(x,y)=7+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(x,y) & ={f}_{xyy}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{xy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxy}(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3})=6\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=3\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b1968108-fe35-55fa-b259-cedf76219668" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489329}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b45857f-4c74-59d9-8e35-85de5f5c9034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488645} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0ab324-a63f-56ba-b911-46caeedd3017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488721} {:answer/id #uuid "88ad4c18-6273-5c23-9f0f-fff73fe14cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488740} {:answer/id #uuid "a2acdb8d-7243-568f-a9db-fdae13525746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488792} {:answer/id #uuid "aed29a6c-7da5-5f41-b70d-1d66d427908e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488740} :answer-field/id #uuid "df0c4030-39f2-5bc9-a0e8-ac2b199792a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c68f1c1a-c6a6-5448-b77c-02d88ae0390d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36482" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{x}^{2}-4y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-4x$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3-5x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=1$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-2{y}^{3}{e}^{{y}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{x}^{2}-4y)={x}^{2}-4 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-4x)={x}^{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3-5x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-2{y}^{3}{e}^{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y-0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'."} :step/id #uuid "be2117cb-2320-5d47-9072-a89c15136337" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489384}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489384 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6926f5e-dc21-5da5-b35d-b718df8a66ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477365} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f6a95f-1511-52d2-946b-1d648665d214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477365} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6da62ee-a826-5879-91f1-9c7966d71193" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477652} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4cb90f6-70fc-53f0-9088-772c160341cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477367} {:answer/id #uuid "26573f3b-930d-57c0-9106-c38022948389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477368} :answer-field/id #uuid "23b11426-2cab-5199-bc57-42df399c747e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477653} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9478de04-8fb1-57f2-973d-3770f1978c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477369} {:answer/id #uuid "a7bf79dc-14ad-5ba5-85de-39e95f01562c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477370}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477370} :answer-field/id #uuid "766dd905-2ba1-5ac0-b3f1-1f815b83a4fe" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3caf1655-4ce4-5a5f-995b-a06e33069b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-65127" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n- **I.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)={x}^{3}y-x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-y$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x+y}-2y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{x+y}-2x$\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-3$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-x)={x}^{3} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}-y)={y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}-2y)={e}^{x+y}-2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}-2x)={e}^{x+y}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x-1})=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."} :step/id #uuid "fdc1b998-1ffd-53d7-9edb-119354e86ad0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489350}}] :activity/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488462}]}
